These modules are the original work of the zig-libs authors, consolidated
here as the canonical home. The authors' other projects depend on zig-libs,
not the reverse. A module's internal development origin — which of the
authors' own projects it was first prototyped in — is NOT recorded here: it
carries no attribution weight and would read backwards once those projects
import zig-libs.

This file answers ONE repository-level question: does consuming zig-libs oblige
you to anything beyond MIT? §1 answers it. It is NOT a catalogue of the
collection's provenance — THIRD-PARTY DESIGN REFERENCES (software designed with
reference to a named third-party implementation, no source copied) are recorded
at the module that has them, in its `README.md` `Provenance:` statement or in a
`modules/<name>/NOTICE` provenance note. None of those carries a license
condition — not because the references are all permissively licensed (they are
not: they include GPL-2.0, GPL-3.0, LGPL-2.1, LGPL-3.0 and AGPL-3.0 projects),
but because a design reference carries no license condition at all. Where
third-party code or data was actually ported or reproduced rather than studied,
its terms travel with it and live beside that code; see section 0 and §1.

====================================================================
0. POLICY — what requires an entry here
====================================================================

A public specification or RFC is not itself a copyrightable work (merger
doctrine: a protocol/format it describes has essentially one correct
expression, and facts/ideas are not protected) — implementing one, however
closely, is not "derived from" anyone's code. Accordingly:

  - A module that is pure clean-room-from-a-public-spec/RFC, with NO
    third-party source code ported and NO third-party implementation
    studied as a design reference, needs NO entry in this file. Its
    RFC/spec citation lives in the module's own SPEC.md ("Verification" /
    design section) instead — see e.g. whois (RFC 3912), rdap (RFC
    7480-family), tar (POSIX ustar + documented GNU extensions).
  - Third-party source actually ported/translated requires its license
    terms reproduced — in `modules/<name>/NOTICE`, beside the source, so
    the notice travels with the code that owes it. Never in this file.
  - A DESIGN REFERENCE — a named third-party implementation that was
    consulted/studied for behavior, algorithm shape, or API design, even
    when no source was copied — is recorded for provenance so any future
    line-level reuse is caught, WITH ITS UPSTREAM LICENSE NAME so that
    reuse would be attributed correctly. These are records, not license
    conditions, and since 2026-08-14 they live AT THE MODULE, not here:
    in `modules/<name>/README.md`'s `Provenance:` statement, or — when the
    record runs longer than a paragraph — in a `modules/<name>/NOTICE`
    provenance note (see §1 for the two kinds of module-local NOTICE).
    Never in this file: a repository-level file that also catalogues 84
    modules' design references stops being readable as the answer to
    "what do I owe?", which is the only question it exists to answer.
  - Exercising an installed third-party binary purely as a black-box
    compatibility test oracle (e.g. running `tar`/`nft` and diffing
    output against this module's own encoder/decoder) is neither of the
    above and needs no entry — no source or design was consulted, only
    the program's observable behavior against its own documented format.
    This covers CAPTURING that output once and committing it as a golden
    fixture, not only invoking the tool live: `tar`'s `write_golden_*.tar`
    are GNU tar's bytes wrapped around a `hello.txt` we wrote, and freezing
    them so the test needs no subprocess changes nothing about who authored
    what. The clause was written for live invocation and this sentence
    exists because two independent readings in the 2026-08-12 audit found
    it did not literally reach the frozen case.

    THE LINE THIS DOES NOT CROSS. The exemption holds when the captured
    artifact is OUR content in the tool's format. It stops the moment the
    artifact also carries content the tool itself authored — a stock
    template, a default stylesheet, a generated boilerplate section — since
    that is no longer merely "the program's observable behavior" on our
    input. `zipstream`'s `xlsx_sample.xlsx` is exactly that case and is why
    the module carries its own provenance note: most of its bytes are
    LibreOffice's default theme and style tables, not ours. When in doubt,
    open the artifact; a fixture's description of itself is a claim, not a
    verification.

