New modules that reproduce third-party code OR third-party data whose notice
must be preserved MUST follow the same pattern: a `modules/<name>/NOTICE`
beside the files that owe it, not an entry here.

A SECOND, DIFFERENT KIND of `modules/<name>/NOTICE` file also exists, and must
not be confused with the twenty-two above: a **provenance note**, whose first line
says `<name> — provenance note`. Those are design-reference records that live
beside the module rather than in this file — the practice grew up around the
self-contained crypto/protocol modules, whose provenance runs to hundreds of
lines per module (`bls12_381` alone is over 800) and would drown this file, and
as of 2026-08-14 it is the rule for every module whose record needs more than a
`README.md` paragraph. They record what was consulted and argue why nothing is
owed; **none of them imposes any condition**, they are NOT listed above, and
`zig build check-catalog` enforces both that the two kinds stay distinguishable
by that first line and that only the condition-bearing ones appear in this
section. Which kind a module needs is decided by CONVENTIONS.md §5, not by
module size.

So: to answer "does consuming zig-libs oblige me to anything beyond MIT?",
read the twenty-two files listed above and nothing else.

====================================================================
2. DESIGN REFERENCES — recorded at the module, not here
====================================================================

NOTHING in this section, and nothing in any of the module-level provenance
records it points at, imposes a redistribution obligation beyond zig-libs' own
MIT license: they are provenance records kept for honesty and for future
auditability, not license conditions. A full provenance/license audit was
completed 2026-07-09 and re-examined 2026-07-29 (both recorded in the project
git history).

WHERE THEY LIVE. Until 2026-08-14 this section carried ~1000 lines indexing the
design references of 84 modules. They now live at the modules that have them:

  - `modules/<name>/README.md`'s `Provenance:` statement — the default, and
    the only provenance location `zig build check-catalog` reads. Each names
    the upstream project AND ITS LICENSE, so any future line-level reuse is
    attributed correctly.
  - `modules/<name>/NOTICE`, first line `<name> — provenance note` — when the
    record needs more than one paragraph, names three or more distinct
    upstream works, or makes a licensing argument that must not be compressed
    (a GPL-exception or merger-doctrine claim). Ten entries moved there on
    2026-08-14: `http`, `icmp`, `ebpf`, `wireguard`, `tc`, `blobmsg`, `dnp3`,
    `opcua`, `rsa`, `fleetsim`. Those files are records; they are NOT listed
    in §1 and carry no condition.
  - `modules/<name>/SPEC.md` is deliberately NOT a destination for a licensing
    record. It is the verification-methodology file, and it is where "studied
    as a design reference" most easily drifts into "verified against" — a
    distinction the records above are written to keep.

A module with no such record needs none: pure clean-room from a public
spec/RFC, per §0.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
UNRESOLVED — two entries deliberately NOT moved (2026-08-14)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

These two were not demoted to a module record, because on re-reading they may
describe REPRODUCED/PORTED third-party material rather than a studied design
reference. If that reading is right, each needs a `modules/<name>/NOTICE` whose
first line is `<name> — third-party attribution` and a row in §1 above — the
same correction `brotli` needed on 2026-08-12. They are left here, verbatim,
until their owner rules. Nothing else in this file depends on the outcome.

  rescue     — Rescue-Prime Optimized (RPO). The permutation, its parameters
               and the constant-derivation rule are public specification facts
               from the RPO report (github.com/ASDiscreteMathematics/rpo) and
               eprint 2020/1143. The 38 published test vectors in that report,
               Winterfell's Rescue-XLIX permutation KAT, and miden-crypto's
               `EXPECTED` digests are used as byte-exact test ORACLES — policy
               §0's black-box-oracle relationship, needing no attribution and
               recorded here only for provenance. Winterfell's Rescue-XLIX
               round constants could NOT be re-derived from the published
               generator and are embedded as a pinned blob; that table is the
               one place where an upstream artifact is carried rather than
               reproduced, and `modules/rescue/SPEC.md` says so.

               WHY UNRESOLVED: `modules/rescue/src/xlix_constants.zig` commits
               that table — `mds_row`, `ark1`, `ark2` — extracted by script
               out of `winterfell/crypto/src/hash/rescue/rp64_256/mod.rs`,
               with upstream SHA-256s recorded beside it. That is upstream
               source content transcribed into this repository, which §0 puts
               on the "reproduced third-party data" side of the line, not the
               black-box-oracle side (the oracle carve-out covers OUR content
               in the tool's format, and stops at content the tool itself
               authored). It is the same shape as `bech32`, `lnwire` and
               `brotli`, all of which carry attribution. This entry also never
               names Winterfell's or miden-crypto's license, which an
               attribution file would have to state exactly rather than guess.

  minisign   — clean-room from the minisign wire-format facts documented in
               jedisct1/minisign's `minisign.h`/`minisign.c` (ISC License) —
               the fixed struct layouts (`RawPublicKey`/`RawSecretKey`/
               `RawSignature`), the 2-byte algorithm tags ("Ed"/"ED" signing,
               "Sc"/none KDF, "B2" checksum), and the untrusted/trusted
               comment file framing are uncopyrightable format facts (merger
               doctrine, policy §0), independently re-derived in Zig and
               cross-checked byte-exact against the real `minisign` 0.12
               binary (Debian/Ubuntu package) run as a black-box test oracle
               (needs no attribution per policy §0 on its own — recorded
               here anyway because minisign.c/.h WAS read directly for the
               exact byte layout, which the black-box-oracle policy §0
               carve-out does not cover). `isPrintableComment` mirrors
               minisign.c's `is_printable` control-character/UTF-8-validity
               algorithm shape — a genuine design reference, ported
               algorithm-for-algorithm (not literally copied C) since it is
               a small, precisely specified check with no other reasonable
               expression. Design ref: jedisct1/zig-minisign (ISC License)
               for the Zig-idiomatic API shape (not read line-by-line, but
               named as this module's Zig-ecosystem sibling per
               CONVENTIONS §5). scrypt parameter derivation (ops/mem-limit
               → N/r/p) reuses Zig std's own
               `std.crypto.pwhash.scrypt.Params.fromLimits`, which already
               matches libsodium's `pickparams` algorithm identically — no
               separate port was needed or done.

               WHY UNRESOLVED: §0 says third-party source actually
               ported/translated requires its license terms reproduced in
               `modules/<name>/NOTICE`. `modules/minisign/src/root.zig`'s own
               doc-comment calls `isPrintableComment` a "Faithful port of
               minisign.c's `is_printable`". The merger argument above may
               well hold for a thirty-line predicate, but it is the owner's
               call, and the precedent cuts the other way: `poseidon` carries
               a `third-party attribution` file for porting a sage script of
               comparable size.
